════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
pack-refs on the derived tick — a controlled A/B, 2026-09-07
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

WHY THIS EXISTS. runce's P12 ladder improved (decay 2.6x → 2.0x) but it
had no in-run control: it compared forge to forge on a different cluster
on a different day. This rig has the control the question needs — the
identical push to a PLAIN bare repository holding the identical refs,
with no hook, no syncer and no bucket.

ARMS — they differ in exactly ONE dimension.
  after  = HEAD (aaffe335): `publish_derived` and `restore` call
           `git pack-refs --all`
  before = the same tree with ONLY those two call sites removed
Verified by CONTENT, not intent: the eprintln marker and every
`pack-refs` string appear in `after` (1 and 3) and in `before` (0 and 0);
the binaries' sha256 differ. ARM=batch, so the treatment fires every
batch. Two interleaved reps per arm, 15 probes per rung.
Pre-registered before any number was seen (prereg-packrefs.md).

──────────────────────────── THE NUMBERS ───────────────────────────────
forge_ms = the lone one-ref push; plain_ms = the same push to the bare
control; forge-plain = what forge adds; usinfo_ms = update-server-info.

  refs   arm      forge_ms      plain_ms      forge-plain   usinfo_ms
  ----   ------   -----------   -----------   -----------   ---------
     0   before   214   213      69    69      145   144     32   32
     0   after    231   229      69    70      162   159     33   34
  2000   before   588   569     313   309      275   260     90   86
  2000   after    376   384     307   311       69    73     34   33
  8000   before  2847  2670    2142  2079      705   591    251  239
  8000   after   1825  1818    2057  2052     -232  -234     37   37

──────────────────────── AGAINST THE PREDICTIONS ───────────────────────

(2) HELD, and it is the main result. `forge_ms` is lower with pack-refs
    at both loaded rungs and the gap grows: 588 → 376 at 2000 (−36%),
    2847 → 1825 at 8000 (−36%, ≈1.0 s per push). Reproducible across
    both reps to within 2%.
(3) HELD, so the comparison is valid: `plain_ms` is unchanged between
    arms (69/311/2080 either way, within ~4%). The control did not move.
(5) HELD and then some: the effect at 8000 is ≈1.0 s, not "hundreds of
    ms".
(1) MILDLY VIOLATED, and worth recording. I predicted rung 0 would be
    INDISTINGUISHABLE. It is not: `after` is consistently ~16 ms slower
    (231/229 against 214/213), in all four runs, with tight spreads.
    That is the cost of forking `git pack-refs` on a derived tick with
    nothing to pack. Small, fixed, and paid per batch under ARM=batch.
(4) WRONG, in the interesting direction. I predicted `forge - plain`
    would shrink but NOT reach zero, because the snapshot and info/refs
    are O(refs) and rewritten whole. At 8000 it went NEGATIVE: −232 ms.

    WHAT THAT DOES NOT MEAN. It is not "forge serves a push faster than
    git". The bare control's refs are LOOSE — nothing ever packs them —
    so at 8000 refs the control pays a cost forge no longer pays. The
    honest statement is that the loose-ref walk DOMINATES this
    workload, and forge now performs the maintenance that removes it
    while a naive bare repository does not. forge's own O(refs) work is
    still there and still visible: snapshot_B 545,613 and inforefs_B
    511,008 are identical in both arms.

`usinfo_ms` is where much of it comes from: 251 → 37 ms at 8000 refs.
`update-server-info` walks the refs too, and packing them collapses it.

──────────────────────────── WHAT THIS SETTLES ─────────────────────────
runce's cross-run ladder improvement is corroborated by a controlled
experiment: packing refs on the derived tick is worth ≈1.0 s per push at
8,000 refs, against a fixed cost of ≈16 ms per batch when there is
nothing to pack. On a repository with few refs the change is a small
loss; from a couple of thousand refs upward it is a large win.

Rig: forge/e2e/refscale/run-refscale.sh, MinIO on loopback, one 8 GB
laptop. Loopback numbers; the RATIOS are the reading, not the absolutes.
